      "latency_ms": 21894.9,
      "prompt_tokens": 25,
      "completion_tokens": 913,
      "output": "\n\nWater is a chemical substance composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom, forming the molecule **H₂O**. It is a fundamental and essential component of life on Earth, existing in three primary states: **solid** (ice), **liquid** (water), and **gas** (water vapor). \n\n### Key Characteristics:\n1. **Chemical Structure**:  \n   Water molecules are **polar**, meaning they have a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side and a slight negative charge on the oxygen side. This polarity allows water to form **hydrogen bonds**, which give it unique properties like high surface tension, cohesion, and adhesion.\n\n2. **Physical Properties**:  \n   - **Liquid at room temperature**: Unlike other small molecules, water remains liquid due to hydrogen bonding.  \n   - **High specific heat**: It absorbs and releases heat slowly, helping regulate temperature in organisms and environments.  \n   - **Universal solvent**: It dissolves many substances, facilitating chemical reactions in biological systems and ecosystems.\n\n3. **Biological Importance**:  \n   - **Life-sustaining**: All known forms of life require water for survival. It acts as a **solvent** for nutrients, a **transport medium** in cells, and a **reactant** in metabolic processes.  \n   - **Body function**: In humans, water makes up ~60% of the body, aiding in temperature regulation, waste removal, and lubrication of joints.\n\n4. **Environmental Role**:  \n   Water cycles through the **hydrological cycle** (evaporation, condensation, precipitation), shaping weather, climate, and ecosystems. It is also a critical resource for agriculture, industry, and human consumption.\n\n5. **Unique Properties**:  \n   - **High boiling/melting points**: Due to hydrogen bonding, water has higher than expected melting and boiling points for its molecular weight.  \n   - **Density anomaly**: Ice is less dense than liquid water, allowing it to float and insulate aquatic life in cold climates.\n\nIn summary, water is a simple yet extraordinary molecule whose properties make it indispensable for life, Earth's climate, and countless natural processes."
